From 62cd1fcb1e05296dd04f28e5c012a6de28ffd208 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:33:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] 3D: the bridge draws a view at a time, profile
 20 goes out in 3D, and how a 3D video goes out

The Dolby Vision bridge (Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10 or SDR, HDR mapped
to SDR by the method of a reference display) drew no 3D picture: it
draws a picture from textures of its own size into a place of the
window, and every way to it refused a picture of two views. So a Dolby
Vision 3D film of profile 20 whose base layer is in IPT-PQ-c2 (no
picture by itself) played one view, in 2D, and an HDR 3D video sent as
SDR was always tone mapped by Kodi's own shaders. Three things, whose
lines cross in the same functions of the window system and the renderer:

1. The bridge draws a 3D picture a view at a time
   (KODI::UTILS::STEREO3D, xbmc/utils/Stereo3DVideo.h;
   WinSystemGbmGLESStereo.cpp). The part of the picture that Kodi shows
   for the eye it draws (CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea()) is cut at the
   seam into the views it covers; each view is copied out
   (dvbridge_gl_part_copy()) and drawn as the same picture in 2D, into
   the place of that eye in the window (CGraphicContext::
   StereoCorrection(), cut to the eye). Both renderers say the packing
   (CDVBridgeGLES::Layer::stereo), and a packed picture is taken where
   each view fits (DVCpuWhyNot()). The views of a frame are timed
   together for the step-down of Dolby Vision rebuilt (one level for the
   frame), a view that could not be drawn is never replaced by the
   picture before (it may be of the other eye), no HDR10+ is measured of
   a view, and the textures are let go with the next picture that is not
   3D. Where Kodi puts both eyes into one picture (anaglyph, interlaced,
   checkerboard) the bridge, whose copy into the window takes no mask,
   draws only the left view of a picture whose base layer is no picture
   by itself (2D), and nothing else.

2. Dolby Vision profile 20 with compatibility 0 decodes both views where
   the window system can do that (CWinSystemBase::
   RebuildsDolbyVisionIn3D(): OpenGL ES 3.2 or the copy extension;
   KeptToOneView()), and goes out in 3D, rebuilt as HDR10 or mapped to
   SDR, as profile 5 does in 2D. FFmpeg gives both views the RPU of the
   base layer, and the packer keeps it on the picture of the two. It
   never goes out natively.

3. "How 3D video goes out" (videoscreen.hades3dcolour, System > Display >
   Stereoscopic 3D, Expert): Automatic (the default), HDR, SDR in
   BT.2020, SDR in BT.709, for those who try what a 3D TV or projector
   takes. Automatic sends what Hades sent before: HDR where the display
   takes the HDR of the video, SDR elsewhere in the gamut of "HDR to SDR:
   gamut of the output", an HDR10 or HLG 3D video mapped by Kodi's own
   tone mapping (by the bridge only with a gamut wider than BT.709, as in
   2D). HDR stands for "Play HDR videos as SDR" for a 3D video. The SDR
   choices map every HDR 3D video to SDR in that gamut, on a display
   that takes HDR too (HdrAsSdr(), SdrMappedContainer(), and the stream
   decoded for SDR as the user's choice of SDR for a video does), with
   the tone mapping method of the video as in 2D. The choice for one
   video comes first; SDR 3D videos go out as they are. The player
   information says in its 3D row what an HDR 3D video goes out as and
   whether that is what was chosen (hades.out.stereo3d,
   hades.out.stereo3dby; the layout in short there), and kodi.log says it
   once for each 3D video ("3D video (...): goes out as ...").

With the defaults nothing that went out before changes: no 3D picture
that Kodi drew before is drawn by the bridge (Automatic with BT.709), and
a 2D picture takes the ways of before, byte for byte (the hooks in
RenderDVConversion() and RenderPlainMapping() are those of a picture of
no 3D video there). What changes by default is profile 20 with
compatibility 0, which plays in 3D instead of one view.

Strings: core 62600-62604, Estuary 54600-54609 (en_gb, es_es, es_ar,
es_mx); system/hades made again by make-playerinfo.py.

Tested on the host: tools/regress/kodi-host stage stereo (stereo3d_test:
the rules, the views at the seam and in the eye, their timing, profile
20, the callers; the rules before fail), tools/regress/dvbridge stage
stereo3d (each eye value for value the picture in 2D at its place:
profile 5 rebuilt as HDR10 in half side by side at 2160p, frame packing
at 1080p and a cropped picture, mapped to SDR in BT.2020, HDR10 mapped
to SDR in BT.709), tools/regress/mvhevc stage p20 (a profile 20 file
made from an ITU-T conformance stream: both views decoded where it is
rebuilt in 3D, the metadata on both views and on the picture of the
two), tools/regress/playerinfo. Not tried on a 3D display, nor with a
real profile 20 file.
